WEBVTT


00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:07.380
Shaping a shallow round neck line
for a sweater back can be tricky.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:13.710
There are only about one inch worth
of rows involved, but we'll knit three

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:18.390
separate sections to create that
nice round shape for the back neck.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:26.040
Although I didn't add a lifeline in this
sample, I suggest you stop a few rows

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:30.540
before knitting your neck shaping
and scrap off the entire piece.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:35.820
Then rehang the stitches and
continue knitting this way.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:40.769
If disaster strikes, you'll be able to
rescue your knitting and start again.

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:43.730
Let's get started.

00:00:44.835 --> 00:00:50.655
I'm going to use short row shaping for
the shaped shoulders, and at the same time

00:00:50.714 --> 00:00:53.985
I'll work simple decreases
for the neck edge shaping

00:00:56.015 --> 00:01:01.144
. I could short row the neck edge as well,
but for this one inch of shaping, it's

00:01:01.144 --> 00:01:03.815
really too easy for me to get confused.

00:01:04.235 --> 00:01:09.755
Now, you may not have this problem and I
suggest you try short rows on both sides.

00:01:09.755 --> 00:01:10.475
If you like.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:18.330
I'm on row 54 the carriage is on the right
and I'm putting the center 24 needles into

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:19.170
hold position.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:24.150
I'll also hold the left side
needles as well for now.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.980
Double check to make sure
the carriage is set to hold.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.970
My pattern tells me to bind off three
stitches, but because I'm choosing to

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:41.850
short row, I'm going to unknit these three
stitches on this row and wrap that edge

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:42.690
stitch.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:44.730
Does that make sense?

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:50.100
The working yarn is in position to bind
off those first three shoulder stitches,

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.080
but I'm going to hold them instead.

00:01:59.065 --> 00:02:05.335
I'll knit to the left,row counter 55 and
following my pattern, I'll decrease one

00:02:05.335 --> 00:02:06.595
stitch at the neck edge.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:29.500
Now before I knit back, I'll put three
more needles in hold on that shoulder edge

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:34.510
now knit to the right and
I'll be on row counter 56.

00:02:36.325 --> 00:02:37.704
Wrap that end needle.

00:02:37.945 --> 00:02:39.295
We don't want any holes.

00:02:41.635 --> 00:02:47.665
Notice that I've now bound off actually
held six stitches on the shoulder.

00:02:49.304 --> 00:02:51.855
I'll continue to decrease
at the neck edge.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:17.650
And knit to the left.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:19.360
Row counter 57.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.440
Actually, I decreased
a little quickly here.

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:28.420
We'll decrease another stitch.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:33.550
Pay attention to your pattern, Sue, and
put three more needles in hold on the

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:34.660
shoulder edge.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:39.280
I'll knit back to row counter 58.

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:42.400
Don't forget to wrap.

00:03:45.045 --> 00:03:46.395
Knit to the left.

00:03:47.265 --> 00:03:53.805
Row counter 59 even though my pattern is
telling me to bind off three stitches, I

00:03:53.805 --> 00:03:59.055
really don't want to wrap near that
seeming stitch, so I'm going to only hold

00:03:59.055 --> 00:04:00.525
two stitches this time.

00:04:01.425 --> 00:04:05.295
Sometimes it's okay to break the rules,
especially when you make a mistake like I

00:04:05.295 --> 00:04:06.015
just did.

00:04:09.205 --> 00:04:10.975
Knit across and back.

00:04:23.945 --> 00:04:29.915
Now because I chose to short row, I've
knit one extra row over just a few of

00:04:29.915 --> 00:04:32.855
those little stitches and
that shouldn't be a problem.

00:04:34.085 --> 00:04:37.265
We choose to short row
to have a smoother seam.

00:04:37.625 --> 00:04:40.265
One extra row isn't going to hurt.

00:04:42.224 --> 00:04:46.575
At this point, notice that there
are a bunch of wraps on the needles.

00:04:47.265 --> 00:04:53.294
So I'm going to net one more row to
secure these reps and then scrap off.

00:04:54.734 --> 00:05:00.765
Now you could scrap off without knitting
that extra row, but when you go to rehang

00:05:00.765 --> 00:05:06.674
this edge to seam the shoulder, you'll
have stitches and wraps to pick up at the

00:05:06.674 --> 00:05:07.395
same time.

00:05:07.575 --> 00:05:09.435
This is not my idea of fun.

00:05:10.095 --> 00:05:17.115
Knit an extra row rehang the stitches for
the shoulder seam and take out that row.

00:05:17.115 --> 00:05:17.805
If it bothers.

00:05:17.805 --> 00:05:21.945
You again, just one more row
isn't going to hurt anything.

00:05:24.195 --> 00:05:28.844
You might also want to adjust your
shaping to match your row counts exactly.

00:05:29.385 --> 00:05:32.984
If you're that kind of knitter and you
must have your row counts exact to the

00:05:32.984 --> 00:05:34.245
pattern, go for it.

00:05:35.025 --> 00:05:40.215
At this point, I'd rather have a nice
smooth seam rather than steric steps.

00:05:41.895 --> 00:05:42.375
Okay.

00:05:43.125 --> 00:05:48.825
We have one side shaped and scrapped off
and the other stitches are still in hold.

00:05:52.859 --> 00:05:54.960
At this point, you have a choice.

00:05:55.469 --> 00:06:00.870
You can keep your center stitches in hold
while you knit the second shoulder and be

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:04.440
sure to protect them so they don't get
damaged by the carriage going back and

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:04.830
forth.

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:08.870
But I chose to scrap off
those center stitches.

00:06:09.650 --> 00:06:11.840
No real reason why I did it.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:14.450
Just try it both ways
and see what you like.

00:06:29.859 --> 00:06:34.030
We'll follow our pattern again
and work the shaping in reverse.

00:06:35.289 --> 00:06:41.260
Reset the row counter to 54 check to
make sure your carriage is still in hold.

00:06:42.854 --> 00:06:47.685
Push all the needles back into working
position and then following your first

00:06:47.685 --> 00:06:48.974
shaping instructions.

00:06:49.094 --> 00:06:51.525
Put three needles into hold position.

00:06:53.104 --> 00:06:54.375
Knit across

00:06:59.655 --> 00:07:01.125
and wrap that end needle.

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:16.280
Decrease one stitch and knit to the right.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:22.900
Put three more needles into hold

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:28.710
knit across and wrap.

00:07:35.789 --> 00:07:37.530
Decrease at the neck edge.

00:07:39.119 --> 00:07:41.880
Three more needles in hold
for the shoulder shaping.

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:45.880
KNit left.

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:47.610
wrap,

00:07:48.950 --> 00:07:50.989
decrease, one stitch at the neck edge.

00:07:51.109 --> 00:07:52.309
You get the idea.

00:07:52.429 --> 00:07:56.780
Just keep following your pattern and just
do the reverse of what you did on the

00:07:56.780 --> 00:07:57.530
other side.

00:07:59.045 --> 00:08:03.095
Knit that last row to secure
the wraps and scrap off.

00:08:03.765 --> 00:08:05.225
Voila.

00:08:05.955 --> 00:08:08.895
You're ready to continue
constructing your sweater.

00:08:10.805 --> 00:08:15.395
Now notice that because I chose to
decrease instead of short rowing the

00:08:15.395 --> 00:08:21.065
neckline edges, there is a bit of a
stair-step, but for back neck opening,

00:08:21.215 --> 00:08:22.295
this isn't too bad.

00:08:23.835 --> 00:08:30.225
Again, I suggest you try this exercise
both with decreases and short rows if you

00:08:30.225 --> 00:08:32.655
like and see what you think.